<UserControl x:Class="ChronoMock.App.Views.AuditSectionView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    WHAT THE APPLICATION READ, in every state that block can be in: waiting for the report, told the
    report will never come, and the report itself, folded, with its counts in the header.

    🔴 ONE COMPONENT, TWO PHASES. The session phase shows this while the session runs and the result phase
    shows it once the session is over, and the two drawings must not be allowed to drift apart - the lists
    in here are the half of both screens that carries untouchable rule 4. A section with its own states and
    its own set of controls is a component from the start (GUI rule 9), and the second occurrence of a
    drawing is the moment it stops being markup in a view (GUI rule 5).

    🔴 IT KNOWS NOTHING OF PHASES. It binds the session model like every other view and the model says which
    of its three states applies. The one difference between the phases is where the verdict's reasoning is
    printed - beside the evidence while the session runs, under the headline once it is over - and that
    choice is the model's too (AuditExplainsVerdict), not a switch on this control.

    🔴 IT LOADS THE PARTS LIBRARY LOCALLY, like the phases that host it. A StaticResource is resolved while
    this control's own XAML loads, before it stands in any tree, so the phase's local merge is not visible
    to it - measured on the first build: "PartNote" not found. The merge goes with the phases' merges when
    the product adopts the library.
  -->

  <UserControl.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>
      <!-- The local Parts merge STAYS despite App.xaml merging Parts application-wide: a {StaticResource
           PartX} is resolved as this control's BAML loads, and that lookup never reaches Application.Resources
           (see SetupPhaseView for the measured revert). This control is nested inside the phases, so it never
           saw their local merge either - it has always needed its own. -->
      <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="../Themes/Parts.xaml" />
      </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
      <!--
        What the folded audit says without being opened. Chips, for the same reason the speed section uses
        them - a Run cannot be hidden, so a conditional list of them needs punctuation that appears and
        vanishes.

        🔴 FOUR COUNTS, AND THE THIRD ONE WAS MISSING. The two that decide the verdict, then the channels
        the session meant to watch and could not, then the warnings. The unwatched count was left out as
        "not part of the verdict", and folding turned that into silence: a session with a channel nobody
        could watch said nothing about it until somebody opened this section, while the shipped panel lists
        it in the open. A watch the tool promised and is not running is what untouchable rule 4 forbids
        hiding, and a fold whose header does not mention it hides it. The channels left real on purpose
        stay inside, because they are by design and change no answer.

        🔴 A LABEL AND A NUMBER, never a number and a noun, for the reason setup.scenario_available gives.
        A count followed by a noun needs plural rules in two languages, and this header had them wrong in
        both.

        Named, so the layout guard can read each chip's number off the render.
      -->
      <ControlTemplate x:Key="AuditSummary">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
          <Border x:Name="CoveredChip" Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                  Visibility="{Binding HasCovered, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap">
              <Run Text="{DynamicResource session.chip_covered}" /><Run Text=" " /><Run Text="{Binding Covered.Count, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </TextBlock>
          </Border>
          <Border x:Name="UncoveredChip" Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                  Visibility="{Binding HasUncovered, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap">
              <Run Text="{DynamicResource session.chip_uncovered}" /><Run Text=" " /><Run Text="{Binding Uncovered.Count, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </TextBlock>
          </Border>
          <Border x:Name="UnobservedChip" Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                  Visibility="{Binding HasUnobserved, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap">
              <Run Text="{DynamicResource session.chip_unobserved}" /><Run Text=" " /><Run Text="{Binding Unobserved.Count, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </TextBlock>
          </Border>
          <Border x:Name="WarningsChip" Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                  Visibility="{Binding HasWarnings, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap">
              <Run Text="{DynamicResource session.chip_warnings}" /><Run Text=" " /><Run Text="{Binding Warnings.Count, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </TextBlock>
          </Border>
          <!-- The fifth count, for the same reason the third was missing and had to be added: processes
               the hook never got into are a watch the tool could not run, and a folded section whose
               header does not mention them hides them (untouchable rule 4). The true total, not the
               number of rows - the table groups. -->
          <Border x:Name="ProcessesChip" Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                  Visibility="{Binding HasUncoveredChildren, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap">
              <Run Text="{DynamicResource session.chip_processes}" /><Run Text=" " /><Run Text="{Binding UncoveredChildrenTotal, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </TextBlock>
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <StackPanel>
    <!-- 🔴 THE EMPTY STATE, where the audit will be. Hiding the section left 300 px of nothing between the
         controls and the footer on the first render of the session phase - a screen that looks unfinished
         rather than one that is waiting for a report. It also answers a question a first session actually
         raises: the tool has not said whether the substitution worked, and the reader has no way to know
         whether that is a verdict or a delay. -->
    <TextBlock x:Name="AuditPending" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Margin="{StaticResource StackGapXl}"
               Text="{DynamicResource session.audit_pending}"
               Visibility="{Binding IsAuditPending, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
    <!-- 🔴 AND WHEN IT WILL NOT COME. A session that ended before any report - the application vanished,
         the core stopped - kept promising the list would appear once the tool had checked. It never will,
         so the place says that instead. -->
    <TextBlock x:Name="AuditNeverArrived" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Margin="{StaticResource StackGapXl}"
               Text="{DynamicResource session.audit_never_arrived}"
               Visibility="{Binding AuditNeverArrived, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
    <!-- 🔴 AND WHEN THERE WAS NO SESSION. A start that failed before the first heartbeat got the sentence
         above on the result phase's first render - "the session ended before" - and no session had begun.
         The model tells the two apart, and this is the sentence for the second. -->
    <TextBlock x:Name="AuditNeverStarted" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Margin="{StaticResource StackGapXl}"
               Text="{DynamicResource session.audit_never_started}"
               Visibility="{Binding AuditNeverStarted, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />

    <!--
      THE AUDIT, folded, and absent entirely until a coverage report has arrived - an empty audit box on a
      session that has not reported yet would be answering a question nobody can answer.

      Its header carries the two numbers that decide whether the session proved anything. Untouchable rule
      4 lives here: the tool never claims to have covered a channel it did not, so "meant to watch, could
      not hook" is its own list rather than being folded into the uncovered one.
    -->
    <!-- 🔴 OPEN WHEN THE VERDICT IS WORSE THAN WORKS. A reader who sees Partial or Fails has one
         question - which clocks read the real one - and folding the answer would make them learn to open it.
         OneWay, so they can still fold it and it stays folded (AuditStartsOpen says why a vanish and a failed
         start keep it folded instead). -->
    <Expander x:Name="AuditSection" Style="{StaticResource PartSection}"
              Margin="{StaticResource StackGapXl}"
              IsExpanded="{Binding AuditStartsOpen, Mode=OneWay}"
              Visibility="{Binding CoverageKnown, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
      <Expander.Header>
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartHeading}" Text="{DynamicResource session.section_audit}" />
      </Expander.Header>
      <Expander.Tag>
        <ContentControl Template="{StaticResource AuditSummary}" />
      </Expander.Tag>

      <StackPanel>
        <!-- Why the verdict says what it says, then what that means for the reader - while the session
             runs and the verdict word is a chip in the footer, this is the reasoning behind it and it
             belongs with the evidence. Once the session is over the result phase prints both under its
             headline, and the model turns these off so the same sentence is not on the screen twice. -->
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Text="{Binding VerdictReasonKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                   Visibility="{Binding AuditExplainsVerdict, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Text="{Binding VerdictMeaningKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                   Visibility="{Binding AuditExplainsMeaning, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Text="{Binding CoverageNoteKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                   Visibility="{Binding HasCoverageNote, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />

        <!-- 🔴 ONE TABLE, NOT FIVE LISTS. Every function the session saw is a row: its name, where it
             read its time from (a word in the meaning ink), and how many times. The five lists this
             replaces each had a heading and a scrolling well, and finding where one function had read
             from meant finding the heading it sat under - the owner's review of the shipped build read
             the whole as a wall of text (2026-09-21). The rows are folded from the same five lists the
             view model still exposes (AuditRowsConverter), in the order that matters: the readings that
             fail a verdict first, the ones nobody could judge next, the good news last. The table shell
             (the header, the capped well, one scope for the shared column widths) is the PartTable part,
             which the process table under it shares. -->
        <HeaderedItemsControl x:Name="AuditTable" Style="{StaticResource PartTable}"
                              Margin="{StaticResource StackGapLg}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource AuditRowTemplate}"
                              Visibility="{Binding HasAuditRows, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
          <HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
            <ContentControl x:Name="AuditTableHeader" Template="{StaticResource AuditTableHeader}"
                            Focusable="False" IsTabStop="False" />
          </HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
          <HeaderedItemsControl.ItemsSource>
            <MultiBinding Converter="{StaticResource AuditRows}">
              <Binding Path="Covered" />
              <Binding Path="Uncovered" />
              <Binding Path="Observed" />
              <Binding Path="Unobserved" />
              <Binding Path="InstalledLate" />
            </MultiBinding>
          </HeaderedItemsControl.ItemsSource>
        </HeaderedItemsControl>

        <!-- 🔴 THE PROCESSES THE HOOK NEVER GOT INTO, and until this block the screen only said that some
             existed: the family verdict's reason read "the ones this application started without it ran on
             the real clock", the warning under it said "this application started processes that never got
             the fake clock", and neither said WHICH (untouchable rule 4 - the CLI report names them). One
             row per executable and role, counted, under a heading that carries the true total - the wire
             names at most a fixed number of them, and a total above the rows is said in words under the
             table rather than left as a mismatch. It stands between the audit table and the warnings, where
             the CLI report puts it, because the warnings are about these rows. -->
        

        <StackPanel x:Name="ProcessBlock" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapLg}"
                    Visibility="{Binding HasUncoveredChildren, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
          <TextBlock x:Name="ProcessHeading" Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis">
            <Run Text="{DynamicResource audit.processes_heading}" /><Run Text=" (" /><Run Text="{Binding UncoveredChildrenTotal, Mode=OneWay}" /><Run Text=")" />
          </TextBlock>
          <HeaderedItemsControl x:Name="ProcessTable" Style="{StaticResource PartTable}"
                                Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                                ItemTemplate="{StaticResource ProcessRowTemplate}">
            <!-- Two lists, because a renderer row cannot be read without both: the processes the hook
                 never entered, and the engines the session reached. Same fold, more of the truth. -->
            <HeaderedItemsControl.ItemsSource>
              <MultiBinding Converter="{StaticResource UncoveredProcessRows}">
                <Binding Path="UncoveredChildren" />
                <Binding Path="Engines" />
              </MultiBinding>
            </HeaderedItemsControl.ItemsSource>
            <HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
              <ContentControl x:Name="ProcessTableHeader" Template="{StaticResource ProcessTableHeader}"
                              Focusable="False" IsTabStop="False" />
            </HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
          </HeaderedItemsControl>
          <TextBlock x:Name="ProcessesMore" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                     Visibility="{Binding HasUnnamedUncoveredChildren, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <Run Text="{DynamicResource audit.processes_more_before}" /><Run Text="{Binding UncoveredChildrenUnnamed, StringFormat=' {0} ', Mode=OneWay}" /><Run Text="{DynamicResource audit.processes_more_after}" />
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The web engines inside the application whose pages the session reached, under the processes
             that stayed on the real clock and above the warnings - the place the CLI report gives them,
             and the place that reads right: the two tables together say what inside this application ran
             on which clock, and the warning about an open debugging port names the port from here.

             No count in the heading, unlike the table above. That count exists because the wire caps the
             process list and the total can be larger than the rows - the heading carries a truth the rows
             cannot show. Nothing caps this list, so a count would only repeat what is already visible. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="EngineBlock" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapLg}"
                    Visibility="{Binding HasEngines, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
          <TextBlock x:Name="EngineHeading" Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Text="{DynamicResource audit.engines_heading}" />
          <HeaderedItemsControl x:Name="EngineTable" Style="{StaticResource PartTable}"
                                Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                                ItemTemplate="{StaticResource EngineRowTemplate}"
                                ItemsSource="{Binding Engines, Converter={StaticResource EngineRows}}">
            <HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
              <ContentControl x:Name="EngineTableHeader" Template="{StaticResource EngineTableHeader}"
                              Focusable="False" IsTabStop="False" />
            </HeaderedItemsControl.Header>
          </HeaderedItemsControl>
          <TextBlock x:Name="EngineNote" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}" Text="{DynamicResource audit.engines_note}" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The warnings, each in its own box so a run of them reads as separate things to act on rather
             than one paragraph, under the heading that counts them. -->
        <HeaderedItemsControl x:Name="AuditWarnings" Style="{StaticResource PartNoteList}"
                              Header="{DynamicResource coverage.warnings}"
                              ItemsSource="{Binding Warnings}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource CalloutRow}"
                              Visibility="{Binding HasWarnings, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Expander>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
